1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,540
 So now we're going to talk about what you would do

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:05,780
 with a web page once you've retrieved it in a Python program.

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:08,420
 Call this web scraping.

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:10,800
 And so web scraping or web spidering is the act

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,820
 of retrieving a web page, extracting the links

6
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:15,900
 from those web page, making a queue of un-retrieved links,

7
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:18,200
 and then moving on, and eventually the idea is

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,980
 if you had enough time, energy, bandwidth, and storage,

9
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:23,700
 you could find your way to most of the web pages

10
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:26,920
 on the internet that are pointing, that point to,

11
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:30,120
 that are pointed to by other web pages.

12
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:33,200
 And so you might have all kinds of reasons to scrape data.

13
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,640
 You might have a blog that you posted.

14
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:39,040
 You might have, who knows, maybe you put some data

15
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,600
 in a system, maybe the system's being shut down

16
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,900
 because it's being retired.

17
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:46,460
 You can do all kinds of things.

18
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:48,640
 You could write a little thing, I was talking to somebody

19
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:51,540
 who wrote a thing to retrieve something and check,

20
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:53,700
 and then send a text when something changed.

21
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,240
 All kinds of stuff, or you might make yourself a search engine.

22
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:02,280
 But be careful, not all web sites are happy about you

23
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,880
 using a robot to retrieve their content.

24
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,500
 Some of the web sites, as we'll see, demand that you log in

25
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,740
 and they track what you do, and if they think you're doing

26
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:12,100
 something bad, they will shut your account off.

27
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,540
 Other web sites will track what you're doing

28
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:18,140
 without you logging in, but then shut your address off.

29
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:20,780
 And so you have to be careful, you should read up,

30
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:24,000
 you should figure out what sites allow you to scrape them.

31
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,800
 Now I have some sites that I've set up that you can play with

32
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:29,300
 to make it so that it's legit.

33
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:34,060
 So, parsing HTML is difficult.

34
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:37,060
 Some of the simple examples, you could probably write

35
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:40,700
 a regular expression, or certainly some splitting

36
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,640
 and some whatever, and what you would find

37
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,300
 is you would write that code, and you'd retrieve

38
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,720
 your first five web pages and it would seem to work,

39
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:50,000
 and then it would encounter some really weird

40
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,280
 but legitimate HTML.

41
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,540
 Or maybe even sort of slightly broken HTML.

42
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:56,900
 So the web is full of broken HTML,

43
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:58,660
 and your browsers just look at it and go like,

44
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:00,620
 oh wow, more broken HTML.

45
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:01,960
 But they don't put up error messages,

46
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:05,080
 and so people just leave broken pages up.

47
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:07,840
 But your Python program is going to see those broken pages.

48
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:08,740
 So what you would do is you'd be like,

49
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:12,340
 oh, here's a new weird way to do an anchor tag.

50
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:15,980
 I'll change my code, and then run for another 100 pages,

51
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,920
 and like, oh no, here's a new weird way to do an anchor tag.

52
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:21,020
 And the problem is is that you're going to find

53
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:23,900
 a lot of different ways to mess up an anchor tag.

54
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:25,580
 And someone's already done that.

55
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:28,240
 There's a software called Beautiful Soup,

56
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:30,860
 and we have installation instructions on how to use it,

57
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:34,840
 and really what it is is it's somebody just spent months

58
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:37,180
 figuring out all the nasty things that could happen

59
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:41,560
 and compensated for it and gave you a nice wrapped interface

60
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:43,440
 that just says, look, you give me the HTML

61
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:45,800
 and I'll give you back the tags, okay?

62
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,480
 And so it's called Beautiful Soup.

63
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:49,980
 And so you have to install this.

64
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:52,760
 There's a couple of ways that you can install this.

65
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,640
 If you're good at extending your Python,

66
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,360
 you can just extend and install Beautiful Soup

67
00:02:58,360 --> 00:02:59,940
 for all Python programs.

68
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:04,040
 If you can't change your computer's configuration

69
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,720
 because you're on a school computer

70
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:09,380
 or you're using a USB stick or something,

71
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:11,360
 then there's a way to download this file

72
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,800
 that I've created called bs4.zip.

73
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,440
 And so what you do is you end up with your file

74
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:19,600
 called urllinks.py, and then a little folder called bs4,

75
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:24,640
 which is a folder that has a bunch of files in it

76
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:27,800
 from the zip file, and then you can run it.

77
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,640
 And so it'll pull it in and you'll import from bs4,

78
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:33,720
 Beautiful Soup, and that's either going to pull it in

79
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:37,160
 from the folder you do, or if you have installed it

80
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:41,020
 using the Python installer, it will also just,

81
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:42,700
 you don't have to put this file in.

82
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:46,400
 So it's up to you, you can either do it one of two ways.

83
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,340
 So this is a little bit of code.

84
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:50,820
 Now Beautiful Soup is a complex library,

85
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:53,060
 and so just because this looks easy,

86
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,760
 doing things in Beautiful Soup,

87
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:58,040
 you might have to actually read a bit more to figure it out.

88
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:00,320
 But we're going to just read this.

89
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:03,480
 We're going to import Beautiful Soup.

90
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:08,200
 We're going to ask for a URL right here.

91
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,620
 We're going to take that URL, we're going to open it.

92
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:14,060
 URL open, they give the URL and read the whole thing.

93
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:15,280
 That means we're not writing a loop,

94
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:17,040
 we've read the whole thing, that's okay,

95
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:20,580
 as long as you know that the file's not so large.

96
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:23,640
 And then we're going to pass the data we got back,

97
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:25,460
 and this is going to be bytes, but Beautiful Soup

98
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:27,880
 knows all about bytes and all about UTF-8,

99
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:29,780
 and it figures that out, and you just say,

100
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:33,400
 hey, take that stuff I just got and tear it apart

101
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:37,420
 using HTML, and give me back an object, a soup object.

102
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:39,120
 Now the soup object is something

103
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:40,600
 that you can run queries against.

104
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:43,500
 So it parses it, it deals with all the imperfections

105
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:47,000
 and inconsistencies in this HTML byte array,

106
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,160
 and it fixes that and gives that back.

107
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:52,440
 And so there's various things you can do,

108
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:54,600
 and you got to go look at the Beautiful Soup documentation.

109
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,440
 It could be a whole class on Beautiful Soup.

110
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:00,940
 So here's a thing you can do, is this object,

111
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,420
 you can sort of call it like a function,

112
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:05,860
 and say, hey, give me back the anchor tags.

113
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:07,600
 And anchor tags, of course, are the tags that say

114
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:10,760
 HREF equals blah, blah, blah, slash A.

115
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:15,480
 So all of this is an anchor tag.

116
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,220
 And then we're going to loop through the tags,

117
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:19,400
 because there could be more than one of those anchor tags

118
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:22,440
 in the file, and then we're going to pull out that HREF,

119
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:23,420
 and that's what this does.

120
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:24,400
 We're going to loop through all the tags

121
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:26,000
 and print out the HREF.

122
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,140
 So if you tell it to go to drchuck.com,

123
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:32,300
 it will show you the one external link in drchuck.com.

124
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:36,400
 And so I've got an assignment that sort of goes into that

125
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:41,200
 in some more detail, but this chapter has been

126
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,800
 a whole bunch of interesting stuff.

127
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:46,820
 We started with TCP/IP model, and talked about sockets

128
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:49,140
 that are phone calls between computers,

129
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:52,920
 and then how applications, protocols are developed

130
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:54,940
 to say what we say on those phone calls,

131
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:57,960
 and we've explored then the HTTP protocol,

132
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:01,360
 which is probably the most likely thing you're going to see.

133
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:03,420
 And then we played with all this in Python,

134
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:07,280
 and saw that Python is really good at this.

135
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:10,200
 You can write extremely simple and small programs

136
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:13,860
 to do some extremely complex and powerful things.

137
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:16,380
 And again, that's why people like Python,

138
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:19,720
 is because it makes the complex simple.

139
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:24,240
 (jazz music)

140
